The vocabulary
A GENE is a DNA section coding for one trait (e.g. seed shape). An ALLELE is one version of a gene (round R vs wrinkled r). GENOTYPE is the allele pair an organism carries (RR, Rr, rr); PHENOTYPE is the observable trait (round or wrinkled). DOMINANT alleles show in heterozygotes (Rr looks round); RECESSIVE alleles show only when homozygous (rr). HOMOZYGOUS = identical alleles (RR or rr); HETEROZYGOUS = different alleles (Rr). CARRIERS (e.g. HbAHbS) carry a recessive allele without showing it. Mendel's genius was METHOD: he used pure-breeding lines, crossed them, counted THOUSANDS of offspring, and tracked ONE trait at a time. Peas were perfect: fast-growing, self- or cross-pollinating, with sharply contrasting traits (tall/short, round/wrinkled, green/yellow).

The 3:1 ratio
Cross pure tall (TT) with pure short (tt): all F1 offspring are Tt (all tall — dominance hides t). Self-cross the F1 (Tt × Tt): the F2 generation averages 3 tall : 1 short. Mendel's explanation — the LAW OF SEGREGATION: allele pairs separate into different gametes during meiosis, so each gamete carries ONE allele, and pairs reunite randomly at fertilisation. The wrinkled trait never blended away — it was CARRIED invisibly and reappeared. This disproved blending inheritance and founded genetics. His second law — INDEPENDENT ASSORTMENT: different gene pairs assort independently (Module 4's metaphase I lottery) — holds for genes on different chromosomes.$wdthseed$, NULL, 35, 1, TRUE, TRUE, $wdthseed${"objectives":["Define gene, allele, genotype, phenotype, dominant, recessive, homozygous, heterozygous","Explain Mendel's method and why peas were ideal","State the 3:1 phenotypic ratio and what it proves"],"introduction":"Why do you resemble your parents but match neither exactly? In a monastery garden in the 1860s, Gregor Mendel grew 29,000 pea plants and found the mathematical rules behind the answer.","key_points":["Eight terms must be word-perfect","Mendel's method: pure lines, one trait, huge counts","Segregation happens in meiosis"],"references":["OpenStax Biology 2e — Chapter 12, Mendel's Experiments and Heredity"]}$wdthseed$::jsonb, (select id from public.course_modules where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod05$wdthseed$), (select id from public.course_topics where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod05:top01$wdthseed$), (select id from public.courses where slug = $wdthseed$biology$wdthseed$)) on conflict (seed_key) where seed_key is not null do update set title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lesson_type = excluded.lesson_type, video_url = excluded.video_url, content = excluded.content, resource_url = excluded.resource_url, duration = excluded.duration, order_number = excluded.order_number, is_published = excluded.is_published, published = exclud