X-linked recessive logic
Females are XX, males XY. The X carries 1000+ genes (including blood-clotting factor VIII and colour-vision genes); the tiny Y carries few, mostly male-determining. An X-linked RECESSIVE allele (Xh) shows in females only when homozygous (XhXh) but in males with a SINGLE copy (XhY) — males have no second X to mask it. Hence: males affected far more often; affected fathers pass Xh to ALL daughters (making them carriers) but NEVER to sons (sons get Y from father); carrier mothers pass to HALF their sons (affected) and half their daughters (carriers). Colour blindness (red-green) follows identical logic and is the commonest example (~8% of males). Notation: always write XHXH, XHXh, XhXh, XHY, XhY — bare 'Hh' loses marks in sex-linked questions.$wdthseed$, NULL, 35, 4, TRUE, TRUE, $wdthseed${"objectives":["Explain why X-linked recessive traits hit males harder","Solve haemophilia and colour-blindness crosses","Interpret sex-linked pedigrees"],"introduction":"Queen Victoria's descendants carried haemophilia across Europe's royal families — through daughters who never bled, to sons who did. The X chromosome explains it all.","key_points":["Males show X-linked recessives with one copy","Trace X's from parent to child","Notation must show the chromosomes"],"references":["OpenStax Biology 2e — Chapter 13 (sex-linked inheritance)"]}$wdthseed$::jsonb, (select id from public.course_modules where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod05$wdthseed$), (select id from public.course_topics where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod05:top02$wdthseed$), (select id from public.courses where slug = $wdthseed$biology$wdthseed$)) on conflict (seed_key) where seed_key is not null do update set title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lesson_type = excluded.lesson_type, video_url = excluded.video_url, content = excluded.content, resource_url = excluded.resource_url, duration = excluded.duration, order_number = excluded.order_number, is_published = excluded.is_published, published = excluded.published, metadata = excluded.metadata, module_id = excluded.module_id, topic_id = excluded.topic_id, course_id = excluded.course_id;
