The antibiotic crisis
Bacteria evolve fast: huge populations, 20-minute generations, plus plasmid-swapped genes. Every antibiotic dose is a selection event killing susceptibles; any resistant mutant survives and multiplies. Stopping tablets early is catastrophic: you kill the weak majority but spare the toughest minority, which then recolonise — selecting for resistance with your own body as the laboratory. Hospitals fight back with stewardship (right drug, right dose, FULL course), hygiene (fewer infections = less selection), and combination therapy (multiple drugs = mutants must win several lotteries at once). Tuberculosis, malaria and gonorrhoea already show dangerous resistance in Nigeria and worldwide — completing YOUR prescriptions is a civic duty, not just personal medicine.

Balanced selection and artificial selection
Sometimes selection favours BOTH alleles: in malaria zones, HbAHbS carriers resist malaria AND avoid sickle disease, so natural selection maintains the HbS allele despite its cost in homozygotes — BALANCED POLYMORPHISM (heterozygote advantage). Humans also select ARTIFICIALLY: choosing which plants and animals breed. All dogs descend from wolves; maize from teosinte grass; dairy cows give 10x the milk of wild aurochs. Artificial selection is fast and directed (humans choose the pressure), but it narrows gene pools — pedigree dogs inherit disorders, and monoculture crops risk clone crises (Module 4). Modern breeding plus genetic engineering now moves single genes across species (Bt cowpea resists pod borers), raising food-security hopes and biosafety debates.$wdthseed$, NULL, 30, 2, TRUE, TRUE, $wdthseed${"objectives":["Explain why unfinished antibiotic courses breed resistance","Explain heterozygote advantage in sickle cell","Contrast natural and artificial selection"],"introduction":"Evolution is not ancient history — it is happening in hospitals, farms and mosquito nets this year. Understanding it saves lives.","key_points":["Complete every antibiotic course","Heterozygote advantage = balanced polymorphism","Artificial selection trades diversity for productivity"],"references":["OpenStax Biology 2e — Chapter 19 (population evolution), WHO antibiotic-resistance guidance (general knowledge)"]}$wdthseed$::jsonb, (select id from public.course_modules where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod06$wdthseed$), (select id from public.course_topics where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod06:top01$wdthseed$), (select id from public.courses where slug = $wdthseed$biology$wdthseed$)) on conflict (seed_key) where seed_key is not null do update set title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lesson_type = excluded.lesson_type, video_url = excluded.video_url, content = excluded.content, resource_url = excluded.resource_url, duration = excluded.duration, order_number = excluded.order_number, is_published = excluded.is_published, published = excluded.published, metadata = excluded.metadata, module_id = excluded.module_id, topic_id = excluded.topic_id, course_id = excluded.course_id;
