The speciation recipe
The BIOLOGICAL SPECIES CONCEPT: a species is a group that interbreeds to produce FERTILE offspring (horses + donkeys make sterile mules — different species). ALLOPATRIC speciation (different-homeland) follows four steps: (1) ISOLATION — a barrier (river, mountain, island split) divides one population so gene flow STOPS; (2) DIVERGENCE — each side faces different mutations, selection pressures and drift, so allele frequencies drift apart; (3) ACCUMULATION — differences grow in mating calls, breeding seasons, genitalia, chromosome arrangements; (4) NO RETURN — even if the barrier vanishes, the populations cannot produce fertile offspring: TWO SPECIES. Sympatric speciation (same-homeland, no barrier — e.g. different host plants or breeding times) also occurs but is rarer. Time scales vary: cichlid fishes speciated in thousands of years; mammals usually need millions.

Our own story in four landmarks
Human evolution in brief: (1) BIPEDALISM (~6 million years ago): Sahelanthropus and Ardipithecus walk upright in Africa — freeing hands, reshaping spines and pelvises. (2) TOOL CULTURE (~2.6 mya): Homo habilis ('handy man') knaps stone tools; brains expand with meat-eating and cooking. (3) FIRE AND MIGRATION (~1 mya–300 kya): Homo erectus masters fire, grows ~1000cc brains, and leaves Africa. (4) MODERN HUMANS (~300 kya): Homo sapiens evolve in Africa — language, art, agriculture — then spread worldwide, absorbing Neanderthal and Denisovan cousins (non-Africans carry 1–2% Neanderthal DNA). All humanity remains ONE species: any two humans can have fertile children, and our total genetic diversity is smaller than a single chimpanzee troop's.$wdthseed$, NULL, 30, 4, TRUE, TRUE, $wdthseed${"objectives":["Define a species biologically","Explain allopatric speciation step by step","Outline human evolution in four landmarks"],"introduction":"One finch flock reached two Galápagos islands. A million years later, their descendants cannot interbreed. What happened in between is speciation — evolution's creative climax.","key_points":["Fertility is the species test","Isolation first, divergence second, barriers last","Four human landmarks in order"],"references":["OpenStax Biology 2e — Chapters 18–19 (speciation)"]}$wdthseed$::jsonb, (select id from public.course_modules where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod06$wdthseed$), (select id from public.course_topics where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod06:top02$wdthseed$), (select id from public.courses where slug = $wdthseed$biology$wdthseed$)) on conflict (seed_key) where seed_key is not null do update set title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lesson_type = excluded.lesson_type, video_url = excluded.video_url, content = excluded.content, resource_url = excluded.resource_url, duration = excluded.duration, order_number = excluded.order_number, is_published = excluded.is_published, published = excluded.published, metadata = excluded.metadata, module_id = excluded.module_id, topic_id = excluded.topic_id, course_id = excluded.course_id;
