Trophic levels
PRODUCERS (autotrophs — grass, algae) capture sunlight. PRIMARY CONSUMERS (herbivores — grasshopper, tilapia fry) eat producers. SECONDARY CONSUMERS (small carnivores — frog, catfish) eat herbivores. TERTIARY CONSUMERS (top predators — hawk, crocodile) eat carnivores. DECOMPOSERS (bacteria, fungi) digest dead matter and waste at EVERY level, recycling nutrients. Arrows point IN THE DIRECTION OF ENERGY FLOW (food → feeder): grass → grasshopper → frog → snake → hawk. Real feeding is a WEB (owls eat both mice and frogs), but chains isolate one path for analysis. TROPHIC LEVEL = feeding step counted from producers (level 1).

Why pyramids narrow
Only ~10% of energy transfers between levels: most is lost to RESPIRATION (movement, warmth, metabolism), UNEATEN parts (roots, bones), INEDIBLE parts (bark, fur) and EXCRETION (urine, faeces, egested fibre). Consequences: (1) food chains rarely exceed 4–5 levels — too little energy remains; (2) top predators are few (one lion needs thousands of kg of grass via zebras); (3) eating lower (grains, beans) feeds more people per hectare than beef — a food-security arithmetic every policymaker should know. PYRAMIDS: energy pyramids ALWAYS narrow upward; biomass pyramids usually narrow (but can invert — e.g. tiny fast-reproducing phytoplankton supporting heavier zooplankton); number pyramids can invert (one tree feeding thousands of insects). Biomagnification runs opposite: persistent toxins (mercury, some pesticides) CONCENTRATE upward, poisoning top predators most.$wdthseed$, NULL, 35, 2, TRUE, TRUE, $wdthseed${"objectives":["Construct food chains with correct trophic levels and arrows","Explain the ~10% energy transfer rule","Interpret pyramids of number, biomass and energy"],"introduction":"It takes roughly 10 kg of grain to make 1 kg of beef. Follow the energy and you will understand farming, famine and why there are no six-metre spiders.","key_points":["Arrow direction = energy direction","Four loss routes (respiration first)","Biomagnification vs energy loss contrast"],"references":["OpenStax Biology 2e — Chapter 42 (energy flow, trophic levels)"]}$wdthseed$::jsonb, (select id from public.course_modules where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod07$wdthseed$), (select id from public.course_topics where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod07:top01$wdthseed$), (select id from public.courses where slug = $wdthseed$biology$wdthseed$)) on conflict (seed_key) where seed_key is not null do update set title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lesson_type = excluded.lesson_type, video_url = excluded.video_url, content = excluded.content, resource_url = excluded.resource_url, duration = excluded.duration, order_number = excluded.order_number, is_published = excluded.is_published, published = excluded.published, metadata = excluded.metadata, module_id = excluded.module_id, topic_id = excluded.topic_id, course_id = excluded.course_id;
