The carbon cycle
Atmospheric CO2 enters life through PHOTOSYNTHESIS (producers). Carbon then moves through FEEDING (herbivores → carnivores), returns to air via RESPIRATION (all living cells), DECOMPOSITION (decomposers respiring dead matter), and COMBUSTION (burning wood, fossil fuels, bush fires). Oceans exchange CO2 at the surface; long-term stores (limestone, fossil fuels, peat) lock carbon for millennia. Humans unbalance the cycle by burning fossil stores millions of times faster than they formed — raising CO2, trapping heat (greenhouse effect) and acidifying oceans. Planting trees and protecting forests re-lock carbon; burning bush and flaring gas accelerate the imbalance.

The nitrogen cycle
Nitrogen gas (78% of air) is unusable directly — the triple bond defeats most life. Bacteria run the cycle: (1) NITROGEN FIXATION — Rhizobium in legume root nodules (beans, soya, groundnuts) and free-living Azotobacter convert N2 to ammonia; lightning fixes some too. (2) NITRIFICATION — Nitrosomonas convert ammonia → nitrites, then Nitrobacter convert nitrites → NITRATES, which plants absorb. (3) ASSIMILATION — plants build nitrates into amino acids and proteins; animals gain nitrogen by eating plants. (4) AMMONIFICATION/DECOMPOSITION — decomposers return nitrogen from dead matter, urine and faeces as ammonia. (5) DENITRIFICATION — anaerobic Pseudomonas convert nitrates BACK to N2 gas in waterlogged soils (a fertility loss farmers dread). Crop rotation with beans exploits fixation free of charge — traditional wisdom validated by microbiology.$wdthseed$, NULL, 40, 3, TRUE, TRUE, $wdthseed${"objectives":["Trace carbon through photosynthesis, respiration, feeding and combustion","Explain nitrogen fixation, nitrification, denitrification and the role of bacteria","Link fertiliser overuse to eutrophication"],"introduction":"The carbon in your body was once dinosaur breath, ancient forest and ocean limestone. Atoms recycle endlessly — today's cycles are tomorrow's dinner.","key_points":["Four carbon exits from living matter","Five nitrogen steps + named bacteria","Eutrophication chain in order"],"references":["OpenStax Biology 2e — Chapter 42 (biogeochemical cycles)"]}$wdthseed$::jsonb, (select id from public.course_modules where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod07$wdthseed$), (select id from public.course_topics where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod07:top02$wdthseed$), (select id from public.courses where slug = $wdthseed$biology$wdthseed$)) on conflict (seed_key) where seed_key is not null do update set title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lesson_type = excluded.lesson_type, video_url = excluded.video_url, content = excluded.content, resource_url 