Five relationships
PREDATION (+/−): hunter kills prey (lion/antelope, praying mantis/housefly) — drives cycles and natural selection. COMPETITION (−/−): both sides lose fitness fighting for the same resource — intraspecific (two yam seedlings crowding) is fiercest since needs match exactly; interspecific (goats vs cattle on grass) can exclude one species. MUTUALISM (+/+): both benefit — Rhizobium fixes nitrogen for beans while beans feed it sugars; oxpeckers clean cattle of ticks; gut bacteria digest fibre for us. COMMENSALISM (+/0): one benefits, the other unaffected — epiphytic ferns perch on trees for light; cattle egrets eat insects flushed by grazing herds. PARASITISM (+/−): parasite benefits, host suffers — Plasmodium (malaria), tapeworms, ticks, mistletoe on citrus. Grade relationships by FITNESS EFFECTS (+/−), not feelings.

Predator-prey cycles
Predator and prey populations oscillate in linked waves: many prey → predators feast and breed → many predators over-eat prey → prey crash → predators starve → few predators let prey recover — the cycle repeats with predators LAGGING behind prey (classic lynx-hare graphs). Human interference (pesticides killing predators, overfishing prey) breaks cycles and triggers plagues or collapses. Biological control EXPLOITS predation deliberately: ladybirds vs aphids, Bacillus thuringiensis vs mosquito larvae — pest control with minimal chemicals when done scientifically.$wdthseed$, NULL, 30, 4, TRUE, TRUE, $wdthseed${"objectives":["Define predation, competition and the three symbioses","Give two Nigerian examples of each relationship","Explain predator-prey population cycles"],"introduction":"Oxpeckers ride cattle eating ticks; mistletoe strangles orange trees; lions and leopards hunt the same antelope. Every ecosystem is a web of deals, thefts and wars.","key_points":["Score relationships +/−","Predator lags prey","Mutualism needs two-way proof"],"references":["OpenStax Biology 2e — Chapter 43 (community ecology)"]}$wdthseed$::jsonb, (select id from public.course_modules where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod07$wdthseed$), (select id from public.course_topics where seed_key = $wdthseed$biology:mod07:top02$wdthseed$), (select id from public.courses where slug = $wdthseed$biology$wdthseed$)) on conflict (seed_key) where seed_key is not null do update set title = excluded.title, description = excluded.description, lesson_type = excluded.lesson_type, video_url = excluded.video_url, content = excluded.content, resource_url = excluded.resource_url, duration = excluded.duration, order_number = excluded.order_number, is_published = excluded.is_published, published = excluded.published, metadata = excluded.metadata, module_id = excluded.module_id, topic_id = excluded.topic_id, course_id = excluded.course_id;
